 The last row is orthogonal to the plane.  Thus, if $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ is the normal vector, then
\[\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} \frac{3}{14} \\ \frac{3}{7} \\ \frac{5}{14} \end{pmatrix} = 0.\]Hence, $\frac{3}{14} a + \frac{3}{7} b + \frac{5}{14} c = 0.$  Multiplying both sides by 14 gives $3a + 6b + 5c = 0.$  Then $3a = -5c - 6b.$

Setting $c = -5$ and $b = 2,$ we get $3a = 8,$ so $a = \frac{8}{3}.$  Scaling by 3, we can take $\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\ 6 \\ -15 \end{pmatrix}$ as our normal vector.  We can take both entries in a row of the matrix to be multiples of 3, but then $\gcd(|a|,|b|,|c|)$ will be greater than 1.  So this doesn't work.

Setting $c = -1$ and $b = 1,$ we get $3a = 1,$ so $a = \frac{1}{3}.$  Scaling by 3, we get $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.$  This works.

